<?nvs
// The job `examples/queue.nvs` enqueues and expects to succeed.
//
// [ADR 0084](/docs/decisions/0084.md) § 1: a job names
// a *file*, not a class or a callable — the third construct to take that shape
// after `spawn script` and `Core\Socket::upgrade` — and it runs in an isolate
// with a budget and grants narrowed from the context that enqueued it.
//
// It prints nothing. A worker runs this on the same process and the same
// stdout the parent program is writing its frozen output to, so anything
// echoed here would land in the middle of that.
//
// It parks for a beat first, and that park is what makes the parent's
// `claimed 1` a fact rather than a race. `[queue] workers` runs this isolate
// on the *same core* as the program polling `Core\Queue::stats`, and between
// § 4's claim and § 6's write-back a job that returns straight away hands that
// core back to nobody — so the row is `Claimed` only for as long as this file
// runs, and a poll can never fall inside a window that never yields. Sleeping
// is the shape of every real job (a job that touches nothing is one you would
// not have enqueued), so this is the ordinary case rather than a delay added
// for the fixture.
Core\Time::sleep(150ms);

return ['receipted' => true];
